   "prompt": "You are a literature searcher for a blinded study of *Drosophila* ventral nerve cord hemilineages. Read `PROTOCOL.md` first\n(section 1 defines what counts). You have no access to any model, simulation or prediction, and you must not look for one.\n\nGoal: find published studies (journal articles or preprints) other than the ones listed in `known_sources.csv` that **themselves\nactivate a whole hemilineage (or a named set of hemilineages) of the ventral nerve cord in adult flies and report the behaviour or\nmovement that follows** (a paper that only cites or restates another study's activation results does not qualify). Search kind for this session: **by_citation**: search by citation: papers that cite Harris et al. 2015 (eLife, doi 10.7554/eLife.04493) or Soffers et al. 2025 (eLife, doi 10.7554/eLife.106042) and activate hemilineages in adult flies.\n\nUse WebSearch and WebFetch (publisher pages, PubMed, Europe PMC, bioRxiv, Google Scholar-like listings). Never visit GitHub,\nZenodo or other code hosts, and never open a connectome-simulation paper (PROTOCOL section 5). You do not code results here: for each\ncandidate, record only what identifies it and why it may qualify.\n\nWrite `out/leads.csv` with header `doi,title,year,why,hemilineages_as_named,whole_hemilineage,adult_activation,confidence`\n(`whole_hemilineage` and `adult_activation`: `yes`, `no` or `unclear` from what you read; `confidence`: `high`, `medium`, `low`),\none row per candidate (also candidates you rejected, with the reason in `why`), and `out/notes.md` (queries you ran, what you could\nnot access). Session: `search-by_citation`. Write only inside `out/`. Stop when your queries stop producing new candidates.\n"
  }
